<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
	Sample multisell list for Shop Front.

	THE FILE NAME IS THE LIST ID. data/multisell/950000.xml is list 950000, and
	config/module.ini has to say MultisellId = 950000 to match. Renaming either
	one without the other gives you a page whose button opens nothing.

	THE <npc>-1</npc> SENTINEL IS NOT OPTIONAL

	MultisellData.separateAndSend refuses to send a list to a player unless
	ListContainer.isNpcAllowed says otherwise:

	    if (!list.isNpcAllowed(-1) && (npc == null || !list.isNpcAllowed(npc.getId()))) {
	        if (player.isGM()) { player.sendMessage("... only gm are allowed ..."); }
	        else { return; }                                     <- player stops here
	    }

	isNpcAllowed(-1) is the first test and it is a literal contains(-1), which
	gives three states:

	    no <npcs> block at all  -> allowed set is null -> isNpcAllowed(-1) is
	                              false -> GM only
	    <npcs><npc>SOMEID</npc> -> only that npc instance may open it
	    <npcs><npc>-1</npc>     -> first test passes, the block is skipped
	                              entirely, and the list goes to anybody

	There is no NPC behind this shop. It is opened from a command, so npc is
	null and only the -1 branch can let it through.

	The GM branch does not stop the send either - it writes a message and falls
	through - so "only gm are allowed" was never a permission, it was just the
	branch left standing after the NPC test failed. If a player reports that the
	window will not open for them, this line is the first thing to check.

	MultiSellChoose repeats the same test when the player buys, and skips its
	own npc / objectId / instance / distance checks the same way. So -1 is what
	lets the purchase COMPLETE, not just the window open. Getting this wrong
	gives the worst possible symptom: a window that opens and then refuses
	every purchase.

	Why a multisell and not a merchant buy list: the BuyList packet carries only
	item id, price and a stock marker, with no display id, so the client has to
	draw the item out of its own tables. A multisell sends a display id per
	product, which is why the window shows the same icons the page does.

	Prices are in two places on purpose. The prices in config/module.ini charge
	the page's own quantity buttons; the prices here charge the window. The
	module compares them at startup and logs a warning naming any row that has
	drifted, so changing one and forgetting the other is reported rather than
	silently selling at whichever is cheaper. Keep them together.

	No xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation on the root, on purpose: a relative
	../xsd/multisell.xsd would resolve against the module folder, where there is
	no xsd directory.

	EXPECT ONE HARMLESS MESSAGE AT BOOT. The server validates multisell files, and a
	file with no schema reference has no declaration to validate against, so it
	prints "cvc-elt.1.a: Cannot find the declaration of element 'list'" with this
	file's name and the line of <list>. It is a warning from the XML parser, not an
	error in this file: the list still loads, and the startup line from this module
	("multisell 950000 checked, N row(s)") confirms it. The server prints the same
	message for some of its own files.
-->
<list>
	<npcs>
		<!-- See above. Do not replace with a real npc id, do not delete. -->
		<npc>-1</npc>
	</npcs>
	<!--
		These two rows are the SAME two items, at the SAME prices, as the Items key
		in config/module.ini. That is the whole contract between this file and the
		page: the page draws what Items says, this list is what the window sells.

		Keep them in step. Add a row to one and not the other and the shop stops
		agreeing with itself - the page lists something the window does not have,
		or the window offers something the page never mentioned. Startup compares
		the prices of the rows that appear in both and logs a warning naming any
		that have drifted.

		These are EXAMPLES. 1 is a Short Sword and 1146 is a Squire's Shirt.
	-->
	<item>
		<!-- 1,000 Adena -> Short Sword (1) -->
		<ingredient count="1000" id="57" />
		<production count="1" id="1" />
	</item>
	<item>
		<!-- 25,000 Adena -> Squire's Shirt (1146) -->
		<ingredient count="25000" id="57" />
		<production count="1" id="1146" />
	</item>
</list>